(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-physiology-of-vision-quiz"]={"title":"Physiology of Vision — Physiology Exam 2","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How is the refractive index of a substance defined?","choices":["The ratio of the speed of light in air to the speed of light in the substance","The ratio of a substance's surface curvature to its overall focal length","The wavelength of light that a substance absorbs most strongly","The ratio of a substance's density to the density of water"],"answer":0,"exp":"The refractive index is the ratio of the speed of light in air to its speed in the substance; for example, if light travels at 200,000 km/sec in a substance (versus 300,000 km/sec in air), the refractive index is 300,000/200,000 = 1.5. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the difference between how convex and concave lenses affect light rays?","choices":["A convex lens focuses light rays; a concave lens diverges light rays","Both lens types focus light equally, differing only in focal distance","A convex lens inverts the image; a concave lens leaves it upright","Both lens types diverge light rays, differing only in the degree"],"answer":0,"exp":"A convex lens focuses light rays to a point, while a concave lens diverges light rays outward. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What fraction of the eye's total refractive power comes from the anterior surface of the cornea, and why does vision blur underwater?","choices":["About 2/3, and this refraction is virtually eliminated underwater because water has a refractive index close to that of the cornea","The cornea contributes no refractive power at all, with the lens alone responsible for underwater blurring","All of the refractive power, and vision stays sharp underwater since the lens compensates fully for water's index","About 1/3, and underwater blurring is caused entirely by the lens losing its accommodative ability"],"answer":0,"exp":"About two-thirds of the eye's refractive power comes from the anterior surface of the cornea; underwater, this refraction is virtually eliminated because water's refractive index is close to that of the cornea, producing a blurry image. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is a diopter, and what is the total refractive power of a normal eye?","choices":["A diopter has no single fixed definition; the eye's total refractive power is said to vary quite unpredictably from about 40 to 80 diopters depending on the ambient lighting conditions","A diopter measures intraocular pressure directly in millimeters of mercury; the total refractive power of a healthy adult eye is normally about 15 to 20 mm Hg","A diopter is the power of a lens that focuses parallel light rays at 1 meter; the eye's total refractive power is about 59 diopters (since the retina sits 17mm behind the refractive center, 1000/17 ≈ 59)","A diopter is a unit that measures visual acuity on the Snellen chart scale; the eye's total refractive power is conventionally expressed simply as 20/20 at rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"A diopter is a measure of lens power — 1 diopter is the ability to focus parallel light rays at a distance of 1 meter; since the retina sits about 17 mm behind the eye's refractive center, the eye's total refractive power is about 59 diopters (1000/17). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What happens to the lens during accommodation, and what nervous system controls this process?","choices":["Contraction of the ciliary muscle relaxes the suspensory ligaments, allowing the lens to become thicker (increasing refractive power from 20 to as much as 34 diopters); controlled by the parasympathetic nervous system","Accommodation is controlled entirely by the somatic motor system acting directly on the extraocular muscles, with no autonomic nervous system involvement whatsoever","Accommodation involves no change in lens shape at all; instead, the entire eyeball is thought to elongate slightly to bring near objects into sharper focus each time","Contraction of the ciliary muscle tightens the suspensory ligaments, making the lens flatter and considerably less powerful; this whole process is controlled by the sympathetic nervous system"],"answer":0,"exp":"During accommodation, contraction of the ciliary muscle (attached to the suspensory/zonule ligaments) relaxes tension on the lens, allowing it to become thicker and more spherical, increasing its refractive power from about 20 to as much as 34 diopters; this process is under parasympathetic control. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is presbyopia, and what causes it?","choices":["Nearsightedness caused by an abnormally elongated eyeball shape, present since birth","The age-related inability to accommodate, caused by denaturation of lens proteins that makes the lens less elastic","A bacterial infection of the cornea that gradually clouds and scars the visual axis","A disorder of the retina causing the progressive loss of both colour vision and the peripheral visual fields"],"answer":1,"exp":"Presbyopia is the inability to accommodate, caused by denaturation of proteins within the lens that makes it progressively less elastic with age — accommodation power decreases from about 14 diopters in childhood to 2 diopters by age 50 and to essentially 0 by age 70. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does 20/40 vision mean?","choices":["What a normal person can see at 40 feet, this person must be at 20 feet to see — indicating below-normal acuity","What a normal person can see at 20 feet, this person can see just as easily from 40 feet — indicating above-normal acuity","What a normal person can see at 20 feet, someone with this notation needs 40 feet to see equally clearly — indicating impaired acuity","A ratio comparing pupil diameter to retinal distance, used specifically to grade the severity of astigmatism rather than acuity"],"answer":0,"exp":"20/40 vision means that what a normal person can see clearly at 40 feet, this person must be at 20 feet to see — indicating worse than normal (20/20) visual acuity. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Where is aqueous humor produced, and what is its general flow pathway out of the eye?","choices":["Produced by the choroid; flows posteriorly into the vitreous chamber, then diffuses through the retina into the optic nerve sheath","Produced by the lens epithelium; flows anteriorly into the posterior chamber, then exits directly through the sclera via emissary veins","Produced by the ciliary body; flows through the pupil into the anterior chamber, through the trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm, then into aqueous and extraocular veins","Produced by the cornea's own endothelium; it flows through the anterior chamber and drains directly into the episcleral veins, bypassing the trabecular meshwork entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Aqueous humor is produced by the ciliary body (at a rate of about 2-3 microliters/min) and flows through the pupil into the anterior chamber, through the trabecular meshwork, into the canal of Schlemm, and then into aqueous veins and extraocular veins. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is normal intraocular pressure, and what determines its level?","choices":["Normally about 8 mm Hg in infants, determined mainly by the rate of aqueous humor secretion from the ciliary processes rather than outflow","Normally about 30 mm Hg in older adults, determined by the elastic recoil of the sclera combined with fluctuating choroidal vascular pressure","Normally about 10 mm Hg, determined entirely by the osmotic pressure gradient created by vitreous humor production within the posterior chamber","Normally 15 mm Hg, determined by resistance to aqueous humor outflow through the canal of Schlemm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal intraocular pressure is about 15 mm Hg (range 2-20 mm Hg), determined by the resistance to outflow of aqueous humor at the canal of Schlemm; chronically elevated pressure is a risk factor for glaucoma and can damage the optic nerve. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Approximately how many rods, cones, and ganglion cells does each human retina contain?","choices":["3 million rods, 100 million cones, and 1.6 million ganglion cells","100 million rods, 3 million cones, and 1.6 million ganglion cells","10,000 rods, 10,000 cones, and 10,000 ganglion cells","Equal numbers (1 million each) of rods, cones, and ganglion cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each retina contains approximately 100 million rods, 3 million cones, and 1.6 million ganglion cells. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is special about the central fovea (fovea centralis) that gives it the highest visual acuity?","choices":["It has the thickest neural layer of any region of the retina, with ganglion and bipolar cells stacked directly on top of the photoreceptors","It contains a mix of rods and cones at roughly a 1:1 ratio, with blood vessels running directly over the photoreceptor layer","It contains only cones, with a 1:1 ratio of cones to bipolar cells, and neurons/vessels are displaced to the sides so light strikes cones with minimal obstruction","It has no photoreceptors at all in this region, relying entirely on surrounding parafoveal rods to relay visual information"],"answer":2,"exp":"The central fovea contains only cones (no rods), with a 1:1 ratio of cones to bipolar cells; neuronal cells and blood vessels are displaced to the sides so light can strike the cones with minimal obstruction — together explaining why the fovea has the greatest visual acuity in the retina. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How do rods and cones differ in light sensitivity and photopigment content?","choices":["Rods and cones both saturate at the same light intensity, with photopigment content scaling directly with retinal eccentricity","Rods have high sensitivity (specialized for night/scotopic vision) with more photopigment and high amplification; cones have lower sensitivity (specialized for day/photopic vision) with less photopigment","Rods saturate only in very intense light and contain less photopigment than cones, making them specialized for bright daylight vision","Cones are considerably more sensitive than rods in dim light, containing far more photopigment and showing much greater amplification for reliable single-photon detection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Rods have high sensitivity (specialized for night/scotopic vision), contain more photopigment, and show high amplification capable of single-photon detection, but saturate in daylight; cones have lower sensitivity (specialized for day/photopic vision), less photopigment, and saturate only with intense light. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which photoreceptor type provides color vision, and which provides higher visual acuity?","choices":["Cones are trichromatic (three pigment types) and provide color vision with high acuity due to less convergent retinal pathways; rods are achromatic (one pigment, rhodopsin) with lower acuity due to highly convergent pathways","Rods are trichromatic (three pigment types) and provide color vision with high acuity due to less convergent retinal pathways concentrated in the fovea; cones are achromatic (a single pigment) with lower acuity due to highly convergent pathways","Rods provide higher acuity than cones due to less convergent retinal pathways concentrated centrally in the fovea; cones are achromatic, using a single pigment type distributed diffusely throughout the peripheral retina","Both rods and cones are trichromatic, each containing three distinct photopsin pigments sensitive to different wavelengths; acuity differs only because cones cluster densely within the fovea while rods dominate the periphery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cones are trichromatic (three types, each with a different photopsin pigment) and provide color vision along with high acuity due to less convergent retinal pathways (concentrated in the fovea); rods are achromatic (a single pigment, rhodopsin) with lower acuity due to highly convergent retinal pathways, and are more common in the periphery. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the function of the melanin-containing pigment layer of the retina, and what happens in albinism?","choices":["It produces rhodopsin directly for the photoreceptors and has no role at all in preventing light scatter; albinos therefore show markedly reduced rhodopsin synthesis","It has a purely nutritive role supplying glucose to photoreceptors; albinism has no visual consequences since this layer is unaffected","It prevents light reflection/scattering within the eye, maintaining contrast; albinos lack this pigment layer and have poor visual acuity due to light scattering, rarely correctable better than 20/200","It only matters for color perception, not acuity; albinos retain normal visual acuity but lose red-green wavelength discrimination"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pigment layer of the retina contains melanin, which prevents light reflection/scattering within the globe of the eye and maintains contrast; albinos lack this pigment layer, causing diffuse light scattering and poor visual acuity, rarely correctable to better than 20/200 even with lenses. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What happens when a photon of light is absorbed by the retinal molecule within rhodopsin?","choices":["Retinal changes configuration from 11-cis to all-trans, breaks away from rhodopsin (activating it), which then triggers a second-messenger cascade closing Na+/Ca2+ channels and causing hyperpolarization","The photon directly opens Na+ channels without any second messenger, producing an immediate depolarization lasting milliseconds","Absorption of light causes depolarization rather than hyperpolarization, as retinal shifts from the all-trans form back to 11-cis and opens cation channels in the membrane","Retinal remains unchanged, and rhodopsin is not activated; instead the photon energy is absorbed directly by the opsin backbone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Absorption of a photon by the 11-cis retinal within rhodopsin causes it to change configuration to all-trans retinal, which breaks away from and activates rhodopsin (forming Metarhodopsin II); this triggers a second-messenger cascade leading to closure of Na+/Ca2+ channels and hyperpolarization of the photoreceptor. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the pigment protein found in cones called, and how many types exist?","choices":["Iodopsin; only two types exist in cones, sensitive to blue and yellow wavelengths","Scotopsin; two types exist, sensitive to red and green wavelengths","Melanopsin; four types exist, each tuned to a different visible wavelength","Photopsin; three types exist (sensitive to red, green, and blue wavelengths)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pigment protein in cones is called photopsin, a close analog of rhodopsin; there are three types of photopsin (types I, II, and III), each sensitive to a different part of the visible spectrum (red, green, and blue respectively), giving trichromatic color vision. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What two components make up rhodopsin, and what configuration of retinal is required to bind scotopsin?","choices":["Only opsin protein, with retinal bound irreversibly regardless of its configuration","Melanopsin and iodopsin, requiring the trans configuration of retinal to bind","Scotopsin (a protein) and retinal (a pigment); only the cis configuration of retinal can bind scotopsin to form rhodopsin","Photopsin together with melanin, requiring either the cis or the trans configuration of retinal in order to bind"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rhodopsin (visual purple) is a combination of scotopsin (a protein) and retinal (a pigment); only the cis configuration of retinal can bind with scotopsin to form rhodopsin — light exposure converts it to the trans configuration, which cannot bind scotopsin, causing the retinal to split away. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why does vitamin A deficiency cause night blindness (nyctalopia)?","choices":["Vitamin A deficiency causes excess rhodopsin production within the rod outer segments, which paradoxically desensitizes photoreceptors and disrupts the normal cycle of dark adaptation over several weeks","Vitamin A1 (all-trans retinal) is required to regenerate 11-cis retinal for rhodopsin production; deficiency decreases retinal availability, decreasing rhodopsin production and reducing retinal light sensitivity","Vitamin A deficiency only affects cone function, not rods, reducing daytime color discrimination while leaving nighttime scotopic sensitivity essentially unaffected by the underlying deficiency","Vitamin A has no direct relationship to retinal photopigments; night blindness instead results from reduced blood flow to the optic nerve head during prolonged dim-light exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Vitamin A1 is converted to 11-cis retinal within the retinal pigment epithelium, needed to regenerate rhodopsin; a deficiency (e.g., from GI absorption problems like celiac disease or cholestasis, since vitamin A is fat-soluble) decreases retinal availability, decreasing rhodopsin production and reducing retinal sensitivity to light — causing night blindness. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the global significance of vitamin A deficiency in children?","choices":["It only causes minor, easily reversible vision changes, resolving within days once dietary vitamin A intake is restored","It is the leading cause of preventable childhood blindness worldwide in developing countries, causing an estimated 250,000-500,000 children to become blind each year","It exclusively affects adults, not children, since children's higher metabolic vitamin A turnover protects against deficiency","It has no significant public health impact at all, since fortified foods across the developing world have largely eliminated deficiency blindness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Vitamin A deficiency is the leading cause of preventable childhood blindness worldwide in developing countries, with an estimated 250,000-500,000 children becoming blind each year, with highest prevalence in Southeast Asia and Africa; prolonged severe deficiency can cause total, irreversible blindness. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Why does an object come into better focus as it moves closer to the eye in a person with myopia (nearsightedness)?","choices":["Myopia has no relationship to focal length, since the elongated eyeball only affects peripheral, not central, image formation","Myopia affects only colour perception and not focus distance, because the elongated eyeball alters wavelength refraction rather than the focal point itself","Distant objects always focus better in myopic eyes than near objects, since a longer eyeball shortens the needed focal length","A point source of light has a longer focal length than light from a distant source, which is why closer objects (acting more like point sources) come into focus more easily in myopic (elongated) eyes"],"answer":3,"exp":"A point source of light has a longer focal length compared to light from a distant source; this is why an object comes into focus as it moves closer to the eye in a person with myopia (nearsightedness, associated with an elongated eyeball). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What are the two dietary forms of vitamin A, and what is the most common plant-based (pro-vitamin A) source?","choices":["Vitamin A cannot be obtained from any dietary source and must instead be synthesized endogenously by the liver from cholesterol","Only pro-vitamin A exists, found exclusively in dairy products such as milk and cheese, with no preformed vitamin A in the diet","Only preformed vitamin A exists, found exclusively in animal products, since plants cannot produce carotenoid precursors","Preformed vitamin A (from animal products like meat, fish, dairy) and pro-vitamin A (from plant foods, most commonly beta-carotene)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vitamin A comes in two dietary forms: preformed vitamin A (from animal products such as meat, fish, poultry, and dairy) and pro-vitamin A (from plant-based foods such as fruits and vegetables), with beta-carotene being the most common pro-vitamin A source. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What type of lens is generally used to correct myopia, based on the eye's need to diverge incoming light?","choices":["A concave lens, to diverge light rays before they reach the eye's own overly strong focusing power","Only bifocal lenses can correct myopia, since single-vision lenses cannot diverge light enough for an elongated eyeball","A convex lens, to further focus light and move the focal point even farther in front of the retina than it already sits","No lens correction is possible for myopia; only surgical shortening of the eyeball's axial length restores normal focus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Since myopia results from the eye's optical system focusing light in front of the retina (excessive focusing power for its axial length), a concave (diverging) lens is used to correct it by diverging light rays before they enter the eye, moving the focal point back onto the retina. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How does the adjustability of the lens compare to that of the cornea in terms of refractive power?","choices":["The lens has less overall refractive power than the cornea, but its power is adjustable (via accommodation), while the cornea's refractive power is fixed","Neither structure's refractive power is adjustable; total power instead varies only with pupil diameter and ambient light","The cornea's power is adjustable via accommodation, ranging from 20 to 34 diopters, while the lens's power remains essentially fixed","The lens contributes more total refractive power than the cornea, accounting for roughly two-thirds of the eye's focusing ability"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lens has less refractive power than the cornea overall, but its power is adjustable via accommodation (ranging from about 20 to 34 diopters), while the cornea's refractive power (about two-thirds of the eye's total) is essentially fixed. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What are the two fluid-filled chambers of the eye, and how do they differ in flow characteristics?","choices":["Vitreous humor is in front of the lens and freely flowing; aqueous humor is behind it, forming a gelatinous, slow-flowing mass","Only one fluid chamber exists in the eye, since the anterior and posterior segments share a single continuous fluid space","Aqueous humor (in front of the lens, freely flowing) and vitreous humor (behind the lens, a gelatinous mass with little fluid flow)","Both chambers contain identical freely flowing fluid that circulates continuously between the anterior and posterior segments"],"answer":2,"exp":"The eye has two fluid chambers: aqueous humor in front of the lens (a freely flowing fluid) and vitreous humor behind the lens (a gelatinous mass with little fluid flow). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Who developed the Snellen chart used for visual acuity testing, and in what year?","choices":["Thomas Young, in 1801","Herman Snellen, in 1862","Isaac Newton, in 1700","Hermann von Helmholtz, in 1850"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Snellen chart, used to measure visual acuity via the 20/20 notation system, was developed by Herman Snellen in 1862. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What does 20/15 vision mean?","choices":["The person has worse than normal vision, needing to stand closer than 20 feet to see what a normal eye resolves at 15 feet","It means the person is legally blind, since acuity below 20/200 in the better eye meets the clinical definition of blindness","The person can see at 20 feet what a person with normal (20/20) vision could only see at 15 feet — better than normal acuity","The measurement is not a valid Snellen notation, since standard Snellen charts only pair 20 feet with denominators of 20 or more"],"answer":2,"exp":"20/15 vision means the person can see at 20 feet what a person with standard 20/20 vision could only see at 15 feet — indicating better than normal visual acuity. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"At approximately what rate is aqueous humor produced by the ciliary body?","choices":["2-3 microliters/min (about 3-4 mL/day)","Impossible to quantify with current methods","About 5 mL per day, produced only at night","1 microliter per hour, at most"],"answer":0,"exp":"Aqueous humor is produced by the ciliary body at a rate of about 2-3 microliters per minute, amounting to roughly 3-4 mL per day. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What long-term condition is identified as a risk factor for glaucoma, related to intraocular pressure?","choices":["Long-term (systemic) hypertension, which can increase the rate of aqueous humor production","Long-term hypotension, which reduces ciliary body perfusion and slows aqueous humor turnover","Chronic accommodation of the lens, which gradually stiffens the ciliary muscle and elevates pressure","Excessive vitamin A intake, which thickens the trabecular meshwork and blocks normal outflow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Long-term systemic hypertension is identified as a risk factor for glaucoma, since it can increase the rate of aqueous humor production, and chronically elevated intraocular pressure can damage the optic nerve axons and blood vessels, potentially causing blindness. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why doesn't the presence of neural elements in front of the photoreceptors significantly degrade the retinal image?","choices":["Because neural elements are located entirely behind the photoreceptors, so light reaches rods and cones without obstruction","Because light never actually passes through neural elements in the retina, traveling instead through vascular channels around them","Because only cones are affected by the neural layer, while rods sit in a separate plane entirely unobstructed by overlying cells","Because the neural elements are virtually transparent in vivo, even though light must pass through them to reach the rods and cones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Light must pass through the retina's neural elements before reaching the light-sensitive rods and cones, but this doesn't significantly degrade image quality because the neural elements are virtually transparent in vivo. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Besides preventing light scattering, what important substance does the pigment layer of the retina store in high levels?","choices":["Glycogen, stored as an energy reserve for photoreceptors","Glucose, used for rapid ATP production in the dark","Vitamin A, needed for phototransduction","Sodium, maintaining osmotic balance across the retina"],"answer":2,"exp":"In addition to containing melanin (which prevents light scattering), the pigment layer of the retina contains high levels of vitamin A, which is needed for phototransduction (regeneration of retinal for rhodopsin/photopsin). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
